     "text": "```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import Ridge\nfrom sklearn.base import BaseEstimator, RegressorMixin\nfrom sklearn.utils.validation import check_X_y, check_array, check_is_fitted\nfrom sklearn.exceptions import NotFittedError\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error\nfrom sklearn.utils import resample\nfrom sklearn.utils._param_validation import validate_parameter\n\n\nclass KernelRidgeRegression(BaseEstimator, RegressorMixin):\n    \"\"\"\n    Kernel Ridge Regression is a variant of ridge regression that uses a kernel function to transform the input data\n    before applying the ridge regression algorithm. This allows the model to capture non-linear relationships\n    in the data while maintaining the regularization properties of ridge regression.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    alpha : float, default=1.0\n        The strength of the regularization. Larger values of alpha result in stronger regularization.\n\n    kernel : str, default='linear'\n        The kernel function to use. Supported kernels are 'linear', 'rbf', 'poly', and 'sigmoid'.\n\n    gamma : float, default=0.0\n        The kernel coefficient for 'rbf', 'poly', and 'sigmoid' kernels. For 'linear' kernel, this parameter is ignored.\n\n    degree : int, default=3\n        The degree of the polynomial kernel. Only applicable for 'poly' kernel.\n\n    coef0 : float, default=0.0\n        The independent term in the polynomial and sigmoid kernels. Only applicable for 'poly' and 'sigmoid' kernels.\n\n    epsilon : float, default=0.0\n        The epsilon tolerance for the kernel ridge regression. This is used to determine the effective rank of the kernel matrix.\n\n    Attributes\n    ----------\n    X_ : array-like, shape (n_samples, n_features)\n        The input data used to fit the model.\n\n    y_ : array-like, shape (n_samples,)\n        The target values used to fit the model.\n\n    coef_ : array-like, shape (n_features,)\n        The coefficients of the linear model.\n\n    intercept_ : float\n        The intercept of the model.\n\n    n_features_in_ : int\n        The number of features in the training data.\n\n    _is_fitted : bool\n        Whether the model has been fitted.\n\n    Methods\n    -------\n    fit(X, y)\n        Fit the model to the training data.\n\n    predict(X)\n        Predict the target values for the input data.\n\n    score(X, y)\n        Return the coefficient of determination R",
