     "text": "```python\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import Ridge\nfrom sklearn.base import BaseEstimator, RegressorMixin\nfrom sklearn.utils.validation import check_X_y, check_array, check_is_fitted\nfrom sklearn.exceptions import NotFittedError\nfrom sklearn.utils import column_name_to_index\nimport numpy as np\nimport warnings\n\n@check_array(estimator=None, y=None, ensure_min_samples=2)\n@check_X_y\nclass KernelRidgeRegression(BaseEstimator, RegressorMixin):\n    \"\"\"\n    Kernel Ridge Regression is a variant of ridge regression that uses a kernel function to transform the input data\n    before applying the ridge regression algorithm. This allows the model to capture non-linear relationships\n    in the data while maintaining the regularization properties of ridge regression.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    alpha : float, default=1.0\n        The strength of the regularization. Larger values of alpha result in stronger regularization.\n\n    kernel : str, default='linear'\n        The kernel function to use. Supported kernels include 'linear', 'rbf', 'poly', and 'sigmoid'.\n\n    gamma : float, default=0.5\n        The kernel coefficient for 'rbf', 'poly', and 'sigmoid' kernels. It controls the reach of the kernel\n        and is inversely proportional to the bandwidth of the kernel.\n\n    degree : int, default=3\n        The degree of the polynomial kernel. Only applicable if the kernel is 'poly'.\n\n    coef0 : float, default=0.0\n        The independent term in the polynomial kernel. Only applicable if the kernel is 'poly'.\n\n    epsilon : float, default=0.0\n        Epsilon used in the epsilon-SVR formulation. Only applicable if the kernel is 'rbf'.\n\n    Attributes\n    ----------\n    coef_ : array-like, shape (n_features,)\n        The coefficients of the ridge regression model.\n\n    intercept_ : float\n        The intercept of the model.\n\n    _kernel : str\n        The kernel function used by the model.\n\n    _gamma : float\n        The kernel coefficient used by the model.\n\n    _degree : int\n        The degree of the polynomial kernel used by the model.\n\n    _coef0 : float\n        The independent term in the polynomial kernel used by the model.\n\n    _epsilon : float\n        The epsilon used in the epsilon-SVR formulation.\n\n    _X : array-like, shape (n_samples, n_features)\n        The input data used to fit the model.\n\n    _y : array",
